<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  centrion's listener as a Task Scheduler task. WINDOWS.md section 7, slice W5b. The analogue of
  launchd/com.tommy.centrion.bot.plist, and the four places it differs are all here.

  1. There is no KeepAlive. launchd restarts the job itself; the scheduler runs an action
     once and is finished, so the restart loop is written down in windows\bot.cmd and the
     action is that batch file rather than bot.py. RestartOnFailure below is not the same
     thing - it is for the wrapper dying, three tries a minute apart, where bot.cmd's loop
     is unconditional and forever.

  2. There is no StandardOutPath. launchd opens the log itself, before the job runs; the
     scheduler opens nothing at all, and the whole of SPEC.md section 14's first diagnostic is the
     append redirections inside bot.cmd. Nothing in this file names var\bot.log, on purpose:
     a path here would be a comment that goes stale rather than a setting that does anything.

  3. There is no environment. The plist sets PYTHONUNBUFFERED and a PATH; the task schema
     has no element for either, measured (W5b) rather than assumed, so a task cannot set an
     environment variable at all. Neither is needed - bot.log() writes to stderr and flushes
     every line, and every Windows session has a usable PATH. PYTHONIOENCODING, which section 9's
     W5b hoped to put here, is therefore not something this file can decide; see bot.cmd.

  4. Priority 5, NOT the default. This is the setting to get wrong, and it is the plist's
     "ProcessType Standard, NOT Background" with the numbers changed. A task created without
     a <Priority> gets 7, which is BELOW_NORMAL_PRIORITY_CLASS, and a spawned process
     inherits its parent's priority class - so every Claude Code session started from the
     phone would run below normal for as long as it lived. Measured on this box (W5b): the
     listener comes up at BELOW_NORMAL with no Priority element and at NORMAL with this one.
     4, 5 and 6 are all NORMAL_PRIORITY_CLASS; 5 is the middle of them. The symptom of
     getting it wrong is "Remote Control feels slow", days later, with nothing in any log -
     which is word for word what the plist says about ProcessType, because it is the same bug.

  __CHECKOUT__ and __USER__ are placeholders, not values. The checkout for the plist's
  reason - this repo lives under a different path on every box, and neither launchd nor the
  scheduler expands a ~. The user for a reason the Mac does not have: a LaunchAgent is this
  user's because of the directory it is installed into, where a task lives in one
  machine-global store, and a LogonTrigger with no UserId means *any* user's logon, which
  schtasks refuses with "Access is denied" unless it is run elevated (measured, W5b).
  windows\install.ps1 writes both in.

  install:  powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File windows\install.ps1
  print:    powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File windows\install.ps1 -Print
  status:   schtasks /Query /TN centrion /V /FO LIST
  start:    schtasks /Run /TN centrion
  stop:     schtasks /End /TN centrion
  remove:   schtasks /Delete /TN centrion /F
  logs:     type var\bot.log

  The prolog is bare on purpose and this file must stay ASCII with no byte order mark.
  `schtasks /Create /XML` refuses encoding="UTF-8" outright ("unable to switch the
  encoding") and refuses a UTF-8 BOM as "incorrect document syntax" at (1,2); it accepts
  encoding="UTF-16", but then Python's expat will not parse the file unless the bytes really
  are UTF-16, and UTF-16 in a repository is a file git diffs as binary. All measured, W5b.
-->
<Task version="1.2" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/windows/2004/02/mit/task">
  <RegistrationInfo>
    <URI>\centrion</URI>
    <Author>__USER__</Author>
    <Description>centrion: long-polls Telegram and starts Claude Code sessions on this box (SPEC.md 8).</Description>
  </RegistrationInfo>

  <Triggers>
    <!-- The plist's RunAtLoad. InteractiveToken means the bot exists only while this user is
         logged on, which is where launchd's GUI domain leaves the Mac too (SPEC.md section 14: "a
         login after a cold boot is still unwatched"). W5d is the slice that tries the other
         way, and it is optional. -->
    <LogonTrigger>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
      <UserId>__USER__</UserId>
    </LogonTrigger>
  </Triggers>

  <Principals>
    <Principal id="Author">
      <UserId>__USER__</UserId>
      <!-- Run as the logged-on user, no stored password. -->
      <LogonType>InteractiveToken</LogonType>
      <RunLevel>LeastPrivilege</RunLevel>
    </Principal>
  </Principals>

  <Settings>
    <!-- The default is 3 days, after which the scheduler kills the task and the loop with
         it. A daemon has no time limit; PT0S is how this schema spells "none". -->
    <ExecutionTimeLimit>PT0S</ExecutionTimeLimit>

    <!-- Belt for W4d's braces. The mutex inside the listener is what actually refuses a
         second copy, and it is the one with tests; this stops the scheduler from starting a
         second bot.cmd whose only job would be to be refused every ten seconds forever. -->
    <MultipleInstancesPolicy>IgnoreNew</MultipleInstancesPolicy>

    <!-- This is a laptop, and both of these default the wrong way for a bot: a task that
         will not start on battery is a phone that gets no reply whenever the charger is out,
         with nothing in var\bot.log because the listener never ran. -->
    <DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>false</DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>
    <StopIfGoingOnBatteries>false</StopIfGoingOnBatteries>

    <!-- A missed logon trigger still fires. -->
    <StartWhenAvailable>true</StartWhenAvailable>

    <!-- W5c's checklist is `schtasks /End` and then `schtasks /Run`; without this the second
         half is refused and nothing else explains why. -->
    <AllowStartOnDemand>true</AllowStartOnDemand>

    <!-- For the wrapper itself dying. bot.cmd's own loop handles the listener. -->
    <RestartOnFailure>
      <Interval>PT1M</Interval>
      <Count>3</Count>
    </RestartOnFailure>

    <!-- See note 4 in the header. Not a tuning knob: 7, the default, is BELOW_NORMAL and is
         inherited by every session the listener spawns. -->
    <Priority>5</Priority>

    <!-- No console window on the desktop. -->
    <Hidden>true</Hidden>

    <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    <AllowHardTerminate>true</AllowHardTerminate>
    <RunOnlyIfIdle>false</RunOnlyIfIdle>
    <RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>false</RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>
    <WakeToRun>false</WakeToRun>
    <IdleSettings>
      <StopOnIdleEnd>false</StopOnIdleEnd>
      <RestartOnIdle>false</RestartOnIdle>
    </IdleSettings>
  </Settings>

  <Actions Context="Author">
    <!-- cmd.exe because the action is a batch file and CreateProcess cannot run one. The
         scheduler expands environment variables in Command, which is how this is absolute
         without hard-coding a drive letter - the plist's /bin/sh, spelled for Windows. -->
    <Exec>
      <Command>%SystemRoot%\system32\cmd.exe</Command>
      <Arguments>/c "__CHECKOUT__\windows\bot.cmd"</Arguments>
      <WorkingDirectory>__CHECKOUT__</WorkingDirectory>
    </Exec>
  </Actions>
</Task>
